1104-./LOGBOOK.md:182:- ROOT CAUSE: r6 built rule **S1** so every cross-platform *summary* sentence is a checked projection of the target rows. It left `verification.assertions` — the **other** prose projection of the same rows — unchecked. Two assertions were already stale under it: **A5** claimed `application-groups` on *every* iOS bundle (r5 prohibited both iOS probe rows) and **A9** claimed *every* development profile carries this Mac (all four iOS profile rows declare `devices = ["deferred — ADR-024"]`). Verdict 07 F1/F2. The 1024-check r7 validator passed because **R27 compared assertion membership and substrings, never scope against the rows**.
1105:./LOGBOOK.md:195:- FINDING: Assertion A9 says **every development profile** contains this Mac, while all four iOS profile rows declare `devices = ["deferred — ADR-024"]`; a row-derived `jq -e` gate returns `false`, exit 1. The assertion must distinguish C1 macOS development profiles from deferred iOS development profiles and eventually verify each profile against its row's platform-appropriate device contract.
1106:./LOGBOOK.md:233:- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
1107:./LOGBOOK.md:242:- DECISION: **Ceremony C1 shrank.** No App Group record is created and the App Groups capability is enabled on no App ID. The convenience argument did not need weighing — it evaporates: C1's `explicitlyNotAuthorized` already excludes all four iOS App IDs under ADR-024, so iOS provisioning *already* needs a later portal sitting, and those four iOS App IDs are the records' only consumers. New rule **R30** makes C1 scope **derived** from what its App IDs require, so growing or shrinking it by hand cannot pass.
1108-./LOGBOOK.md:248:- ROOT CAUSE: r1 concluded "the app cannot reach a stopped provider" from the macOS 26.5 SDK header for `handleAppMessage:` — *"called by the framework when the container app sends a message to the provider"*. That sentence names **who sends**, not a liveness precondition, and the header for the sending side says nothing about launch at all. Apple's published API reference does: `sendProviderMessage(_:responseHandler:)` — *"If the extension is not running, **it should be launched to handle the message**."* One inference from a header that was silent, and revocation was declared permanently asynchronous, `379cpk` AC4 was declared unsatisfiable, and a 60-second startup budget was spent waiting for a seed. **Generalisable: for Network Extension lifecycle questions the shipped header and the published reference are not interchangeable — the header documents the signature, the reference documents the behaviour.** Check both before designing around an absence.
1109:./LOGBOOK.md:286:- ROOT CAUSE: r1 registered `group.works.relux.tunnel` but had macOS claim `262RZ595FP.group.works.relux.tunnel`, calling it "the same record with a team prefix". Those are two different Apple identifier *styles*, not two spellings of one record: iOS-style begins `group.` and is allocated on the Developer website; macOS-style begins with the Team ID and **cannot** be allocated there at all. The macOS literal therefore did not identify the portal record the contract said it consumed.
1110-./LOGBOOK.md:304:- DECISION: Apple identifiers are `works.relux.tunnel.{mac,mac.tunnel,ios,ios.tunnel}` plus a disjoint disposable probe family `works.relux.tunnel.probe.*`, under Team `262RZ595FP` (Relux Works, LLC). Provider identifier is always host identifier + `.tunnel`, so containment is derivable in both directions with no lookup table.
1111:./LOGBOOK.md:306:- DECISION: One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration — no `.debug`/`.dev` variants. macOS system-extension approval is keyed to Team ID + extension bundle identifier, so suffixing multiplies physical approval friction on Holds H2/H3, and `9yp8to` must exercise the identity that ships. Environment is expressed by signing identity class, profile class, hardened runtime, and `get-task-allow`. The single exception is the probe family, which exists so throwaway sysex approvals never burn the production identity.
1112-./LOGBOOK.md:346:- DECISION: Owner-approved strategy encoded in `.spec/decisions.md` as ADR-023 (M0 SSH viability scope, Option A, with the four deferred semantics named and owned by TASK-260728-3cveay) and ADR-024 (macOS-only physical target, iOS deferred, Linux CI nonblocking, Relux Works signing/notary available). ADR-013 re-scoped from a blocking prototype gate to a release-path gate; ADR-014 moved Open → Accepted with libssh2 as the primary engine.
1113:./LOGBOOK.md:411:- CORRECTION: Sparkle 2 removed downgrade support, so emergency rollback is last-known-good source rebuilt at a higher `CFBundleVersion`, not an explicit rollback channel. Sparkle also documents Developer ID key rotation, so downstream tests must enforce the approved product identities instead of asserting every Team ID change is intrinsically rejected by Sparkle.
